# FunctionGemma Router Sidecar — stage-2 decoder of the two-stage router.
#
# Serves the round-2 fine-tuned FunctionGemma-270M GGUF (proven 90.1% overall /
# 0% chat-FP on the 81-case corpus, labs/router-90-campaign) on :11436, CPU-only
# (-ngl 0, ~600 MB RSS incl. the mlocked 278 MB GGUF), for
# services/zoe-data/router_two_stage.py. The brain
# (Gemma 4 E4B on :11434) is untouched — this is a fast-tier FRONT, not a brain
# swap (docs/CANONICAL.md rocks hold).
#
# Install (user unit, like kokoro-tts.service):
#   mkdir -p /home/zoe/models/functiongemma-router
#   cp /home/zoe/models/lab/functiongemma-270m-zoe-functok-r2-Q8_0.gguf \
#      /home/zoe/models/functiongemma-router/
#   cp scripts/setup/systemd/functiongemma-router.service ~/.config/systemd/user/
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload
#   systemctl --user enable --now functiongemma-router.service
# Verify: curl -s localhost:11436/health && curl -s localhost:11436/props | grep r2
[Unit]
Description=FunctionGemma Router Sidecar (two-stage router stage-2, CPU :11436)
After=network.target

[Service]
# Flag sizing (2026-09-27, measured; evidence in docs/knowledge/two-stage-router-rollout.md
# "Sidecar flags"):
#  --cache-ram 64  = cap the host prompt cache. Unset, llama.cpp defaults it to
#    8192 MiB — inside this unit's MemoryMax=1G that is not a cap at all: the cgroup
#    OOM-kills the sidecar long before the cache would ever evict. 64 MiB holds the
#    handful of ~1-2 MiB entries this workload makes (prompts <100 tokens) and turns
#    growth into oldest-first eviction. Never remove it, never raise it past the
#    MemoryMax headroom.
#  --ctx-size 1024 = ~12x the longest routing request observed live (87 tokens over
#    608 requests; p99 72). Was 4096. Do not raise without a measured need.
#  Do NOT add --no-repack: weights would be served from reclaimable file pages —
#    the paging failure the memory block below exists to prevent.
#  --mlock (A1, infra audit 2026-10-03, docs/research/infra-data-config-2026-10-03.md
#    D1) = pin the GGUF mapping. Repack alone did NOT keep the weights resident:
#    the tensors llama.cpp serves straight from the mmap stay reclaimable FILE
#    pages, and MemorySwapMax=0 only protects anon. Measured live 2026-10-03:
#    the 278 MB GGUF mapping at Rss 0 kB (fully evicted), 7.7 M file refaults on
#    this cgroup (~12 MB re-read per request), and router `total time` p50/p90
#    293/450 ms when the previous request was <10 min earlier vs 427/638 ms after
#    a >10 min gap (n=2,384, journald 09-28..10-03; max 1,588 ms, past the 1.5 s
#    client timeout). The leaf MemoryLow below cannot fix this: its effective
#    value is 0 on this host (ancestor slices are memory.low=0).
#    SPELLING IS BUILD-SPECIFIC: this unit runs b9733 (build-jetson-new), which
#    takes `--mlock`; the brain's b11194 REMOVED it (`--load-mode mmap+mlock`).
#    Repointing ExecStart at b11194 without respelling is an unknown-flag
#    startup failure — pinned by tests/unit/test_llama_server_unit_flags.py.
#    mlock failing (RLIMIT_MEMLOCK) is only a WARNING in llama.cpp, so the
#    install check is `VmLck` in /proc/<pid>/status ~= 278 MB, not "it started".
Type=simple
Environment=LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/home/zoe/llama.cpp/build-jetson-new/bin
ExecStart=/home/zoe/llama.cpp/build-jetson-new/bin/llama-server \
  --model /home/zoe/models/functiongemma-router/functiongemma-270m-zoe-functok-r2-Q8_0.gguf \
  --host 127.0.0.1 \
  --port 11436 \
  --ctx-size 1024 \
  --cache-ram 64 \
  --n-gpu-layers 0 \
  --parallel 1 \
  --threads 4 \
  --mlock \
  --jinja
# Startup gate: healthy AND serving exactly the r2 GGUF (identity check —
# a wrong model here would silently change routing quality).
ExecStartPost=/bin/bash -c 'for i in $(seq 1 60); do curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:11436/health >/dev/null 2>&1 && break; sleep 1; done; curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:11436/props | grep -q "functiongemma-270m-zoe-functok-r2-Q8_0.gguf"'
# ── Memory protection ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A PAGED-OUT ROUTER IS THE EXACT SILENT-DEGRADATION CLASS THIS GUARD EXISTS
# FOR. router_two_stage.py gives the sidecar a strict 1.5 s client timeout
# (ZOE_ROUTER_TWO_STAGE_TIMEOUT_S) against a 424 ms p50, so the whole budget is
# ~3.5x the normal decode. Faulting ~600 MB back off the NVMe swapfile does not
# fit in what is left: the call times out, `decide()` returns None
# (router_two_stage.py:291), and semantic_router.py:547 silently keeps the
# SIMILARITY decision instead. Nothing errors and nothing is logged as a
# regression — the turn just gets routed by the weaker stage-1-less path AND
# pays the full 1.5 s before doing so. Routing quality quietly drops off the
# 90.1%/0%-chat-FP config while every health check stays green.
#
# Measured read-only on the live Orin 2026-08-03 (PID 1564, up 6h05m, box at
# 1,168 MB available / 2.3 GB swap in use), with MemoryLow=0, MemoryMax and
# MemorySwapMax both `infinity` and NO local drop-in anywhere:
#
#   VmRSS 411.7 MB / VmSwap 156.9 MB / VmHWM 598.8 MB   -> 27.6% paged out
#   cgroup memory.current 372.0 MB / memory.swap.current 155.8 MB
#
# VmHWM 598.8 MB confirms the "~600 MB" figure in the header comment above, and
# it is a TRUSTWORTHY peak rather than a starved lower bound: VmHWM is only
# 1.05x VmRSS+VmSwap here, so the process has actually reached its natural
# working set. (On the flue sidecars that same ratio was 1.8-2.1x, which is why
# their ceilings could not be sized from it.)
#
# MemoryLow=768M is sized to hold the whole 598.8 MB working set with headroom
# (1.28x the peak — the same margin kokoro-tts uses over its 2,465 MB VmHWM),
# so the protected zone covers the model rather than part of it.
#
# MemoryMax (1G until A1, 1280M now — see below) is a leak BACKSTOP, and a
# ceiling is safe here for a reason that does NOT generalise from the binary:
# this is the same llama-server binary that is MemoryMax-EXEMPT on :11434, but
# that exemption is about unaccounted GPU/NvMap pages, and this instance runs
# `--n-gpu-layers 0`. Verified in
# /proc/1564/maps: exactly ONE /dev/nv mapping, a single 4 KB
# /dev/nvgpu/igpu0/ctrl control page, and ZERO /dev/nvmap allocations — the
# nvidia .so files are mapped (Jetson CUDA build) but are ordinary file-backed
# pages. No weights and no KV cache on device, so cgroup accounting is COMPLETE
# and the ceiling genuinely bounds this cgroup.
#
# The 1.67x ceiling-to-floor ratio (1.33x before A1) is below the usual 3x
# rule and is a documented TIGHT_CEILING_OK exception, for kokoro-tts's reason: the 3x rule
# exists for runtimes whose UNCOVERED allocation grows with load (V8 external
# buffers). llama.cpp here is bounded at startup — fixed Q8_0 GGUF, fixed
# --ctx-size 1024 KV cache, --parallel 1, prompt cache capped by --cache-ram 64
# (the 8 GiB default was NOT bounded) — so the only allocation outside the
# floor is per-request grammar/jinja/HTTP scratch for ONE in-flight request.
# The trade is stated rather than assumed: an OOM kill here is a DEGRADATION,
# not an outage (Restart=always + the fallback above), so the tighter backstop
# is preferred over leaving 2.5 GB of unswappable headroom on a 15.6 GB box
# where llama-server holds ~6.4 GB.
#
# MemoryMax 1G -> 1280M with --mlock (A1). Locked pages are unreclaimable, so
# pinning the 278 MB mapping takes it out of what the kernel could reclaim before
# reaching the ceiling. 1280M keeps the anon headroom the 1G ceiling gave when the
# weights were still evictable (1G + 278 MB), so this is the same leak backstop,
# not a looser one. Live 2026-10-03: cgroup 288 MB (anon 283 MB, weights
# evicted), VmHWM 692 MB. Pinned: MemoryLow + the locked GGUF must fit under
# MemoryMax (tests/unit/test_llama_server_unit_flags.py).
# LimitMEMLOCK=infinity: the user manager on this host already defaults it
# (DefaultLimitMEMLOCK=infinity), but a rebuilt host with a small distro default
# (64 KiB-8 MiB) would silently skip the lock — stated here, as in
# llama-server.service.
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity
MemorySwapMax=0
MemoryLow=768M
MemoryMax=1280M
Restart=always
RestartSec=5
TimeoutStartSec=120
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=functiongemma-router

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
